"""Version 3: a more distinctive, reflective voice within the same scientific claims."""
EDITS = {
0: r"""A field comparison can survive repeated article selection and still reverse when the embedding model changes. This study examines that problem using ten models and a fixed corpus of 500,000 OpenAlex records from 26 fields and 2000--2024. Matched panels compare field centres, internal relationships and nearest neighbours, with checks on text input and representation settings. The models share broad structure, yet retain only 30.3\% of the 25 nearest neighbours on average within field-period groups. Removing abstracts changes neighbours about as much as changing models; removing titles changes less. A later exploratory analysis examines all 325 field pairs. Persistent opposite orderings across models occur in 262 pairs for angular spread and 221 for effective linear dimension; requiring relative differences above 5\% leaves 96 and 177. Alternative measures preserve many oppositions, although centring and pooling affect the conclusions. The results describe geometric dependence, without establishing thematic correctness. I argue for following a claim through the choices that produce its map. Repeating a result under article selection answers a useful question, but it does not establish that another representation of the same papers supports the same interpretation.""",
1: r"""Changing a document representation can reverse a comparison between research fields while the articles stay the same. That is the difficulty behind this study. A science map is useful because it turns a collection of papers into relationships that can be inspected: nearby work, neighbouring areas and contrasts between disciplines. With text embeddings, a model first translates each paper into a vector. The distances and arrangements available for interpretation come from those vectors. Applications to biomedical literature and physics show what this makes possible \citep{refbe130e2973,refc0e2cc29e0}. I ask how far a conclusion drawn from such a map travels when the embedding model changes.""",
2: r"""The difficulty is older than these encoders. \citet{refa0d2d707e6} asked what a reconstructed thematic structure owes to scientific knowledge and what it owes to the method of reconstruction. \citet{ref6a2f5912bc} found that text, citation and hybrid maps could have similar aggregate accuracy while assigning substantially different papers to their clusters. Those results locate the problem in the interpretation of a comparison. A favourable global score does not tell a reader whether the particular grouping they want to use survives a change of method.""",
3: r"""I examine this with 500,000 publications labelled English in OpenAlex, published in 2000--2024 and assigned to 26 broad Fields. The papers remain fixed across ten embedding models. Field centres, relationships within fields and individual article neighbours provide successive objects of comparison. Matched subsets then change the text supplied and the rule that turns model output into one vector per paper. Later, an exploratory geometric extension follows a more pointed question: whether two fields retain their ordering when the representation changes, after checking what happens when the articles change.""",
4: r"""The models do share broad relationships. The local comparisons and geometric field orderings show how much work remains before using that agreement to support a particular claim. One model can repeatedly give one ordering as articles change, while another repeatedly gives the reverse. I treat the size of that difference and its response to processing as part of the result. A direction can be persistent and its magnitude small. The comparisons reveal which relationships depend on the choices tested; deciding whether a relationship is substantively right requires another kind of validation.""",
5: r"""Earlier evaluations are useful precisely because they state what a representation is being asked to do. \citet{refc0e2cc29e0} compared text and graph representations with a hierarchical classification of physics papers, including inputs based on titles and abstracts. In a biomedical literature map, \citet{refbe130e2973} compared models and output-reduction rules through the field labels shared by nearby papers. These are concrete evaluation criteria. They clarify why agreement across models, which is the concern here, cannot silently take the place of an external assessment.""",
6: r"""There is also more than an encoder inside a document representation. Someone has to decide which text it receives, how much of that text fits and, for some models, how its token outputs become a single vector. Pooling is this last decision: taking an average and selecting a designated output position need not preserve the same relationships. The sensitivity to short queries and wording changes documented by \citet{singh-singh-2022-inefficiency} makes the input issue tangible. I include these choices because a model comparison otherwise leaves part of its own procedure unexamined.""",
7: r"""The joint comparison of geometry and neighbours is already part of the literature. \citet{ref4e1e0d5582} used it across retrieval datasets, and \citet{ref6da178065c} examined density and asymmetry across document embeddings and disciplines in relation to citations. The question I pursue is what happens to broad, local and geometric conclusions when those comparisons are made within a fixed panel. Matched articles, explicit text and representation conditions, and controlled neighbour candidates keep each result attached to the change actually tested.""",
8: r"""I avoid treating robustness as a property that can be assigned to the whole map in one step. Agreement asks what two representations share on the same papers. Stability asks whether a given result survives a given change. A stable ordering within each model can still be an ordering on which the models disagree. Thematic validity goes further and asks how those relationships correspond to the research itself. In invasion biology, \citet{QSSreviewQ17} showed why interpreting algorithmic groups requires attention to the relationships a mapping method combines. A repeated answer or agreement between models cannot do that interpretive work alone.""",
9: r"""The word \emph{shape} needs similar care. Here it refers to relationships in the embedding spaces before projection into two dimensions. Centres summarise the arrangement of fields; articles retain their internal organisation; neighbours identify the papers closest to a particular article. Angular spread describes the separation of vectors, and effective linear dimension describes how variation occupies directions. Each is a limited view of the representation. Calling a field more spread out does not establish that its topics are more diverse; assigning it more effective dimensions does not count its topics. Keeping the object explicit makes a change in shape interpretable.""",
11: r"""These questions concern the corpus and models actually compared. The third was added after the initial results, in the exploratory extension documented in the methods and supplement. I retain that order of development. OpenAlex labels organise the comparisons and remain in place with their known errors; they are not an independent thematic reference. The study can establish persistence within this design. It cannot identify a single correct map and then use that map to declare a winning model.""",
14: r"""Using the same papers is a starting condition; the different questions also need different panels (Table~\ref{tab:T01}). Three text inputs are compared on 52,000 articles balanced by Field and period, including the earlier 26,000-article pilot. A separate 52,000-article panel tests native inputs against a common literal fragment that fits every tokenizer. Equal-size group comparisons and matched neighbour searches control further changes in the comparison. Every model comparison retains the same article identities. Supplement S1 gives the extraction and cleaning rules, the counts and the flags that remain.""",
15: r"""I chose a fixed panel of ten models to compare representations used for scholarly material across different training approaches (Table~\ref{tab:T02}). It includes scientific-document, general sentence and word-level biomedical/scientific models. Its purpose is a bounded comparison; it cannot represent a random population of encoders or rank the latest releases. Checkpoints, tokenizer revisions and text formats were frozen before inference. Models represented the same titles and abstracts with their customary format and native limit. MiniLM's 256-token setting is primary, and a separate 512-token variant checks its sensitivity. Native limits truncated 49.58\% of MiniLM inputs and 20.16\% of MPNet inputs.""",
18: r"""A centre compresses the unit-length article vectors of a group into their mean. That is the object used to compare broad organisation. The within-group analysis keeps the articles separate. I use random groups to examine how much centre agreement can accompany the act of averaging without the observed labels. Group sizes and publication periods are preserved, and every model receives the same random allocation.""",
22: r"""For Field geometry, I follow all unordered pairs of the 26 Fields: 325 comparisons for each property. A model supports a persistent direction only if that direction survives the primary 2,000 articles per Field, twenty half samples of 1,000 and five additional selections of 2,000 from the same corpus. All-ten agreement requires the same persistent direction throughout the panel. Opposition requires at least two fixed models that repeatedly support opposite directions. The remaining pairs are unresolved, which leaves equality unestablished. To distinguish a change of direction from the size of a difference, relative magnitude is $2(B-A)/(A+B)$, where $A$ and $B$ are the Field values. Minima of 0, 1, 5 and 10\% test sensitivity to small differences.""",
23: r"""These repetitions stay inside one frozen corpus. They test sensitivity to article selection; their overlapping subsets supply neither independent population samples nor confidence intervals. I retain excessive median shifts and wide selection ranges as alerts, with the operational rules and denominators recorded separately in the supplement. Pair counts need the same restraint. Models recur across model pairs, and Fields recur across Field pairs, so these counts are descriptive. No significance test treats the pairs as independent observations.""",
24: r"""The sequence of the work is part of its interpretation. The initial shape and neighbour comparisons came before the larger input panel, matched Subfield searches and case atlas. Morphology was a later exploratory extension, and its all-pairs summary followed the first examples. Rules were recorded locally before the corresponding new calculations. This record is not an external preregistration of the study as a whole. Random-group centres and the fixed-neighbour-fraction check were among controls added after earlier results. Supplement S2 records the sequence, and Supplements S3--S7 retain the time, composition, model-trait, source-error and processing checks, including those that narrow what can be concluded.""",
26: r"""The internal organisation has a more modest level of agreement: mean CKA is around 0.63 for Fields and 0.64 for the same 183 Subfields eligible at every size (Figure~\ref{fig:scales}B). Those averages move little between 128 and 512 articles. A finer disciplinary label thus does not automatically yield a less consistent comparison. There is a large gap from the centre panel, but its interpretation stops short of a causal effect of scale. Averaged centres and their individual articles are different objects.""",
30: r"""Shape and neighbour agreement do move together: the original 45 model-pair means have a descriptive rank correlation of 0.920. I keep both because the interpretation changes with the object. CKA summarises relationships spread through the space. A neighbour list names the papers a reader would meet there. Their association does not make those consequences interchangeable, nor does reporting both create independent evidence of reliability.""",
32: r"""The text supplied to the model deserves as much attention as the model name. On the same 52,000 papers, changing encoders gives a mean shape change of 0.370; retaining the encoder and supplying only the title gives 0.366, with change measured as one minus corrected CKA. The corresponding fractions of neighbours replaced are 68.8\% and 70.7\% (Figure~\ref{fig:input}). An input choice can therefore have consequences of comparable size in this design. The proximity of the averages leaves equivalence untested.""",
34: r"""Even the ordering of those average effects depends on the readout. With the principal mean rule, removing the title has a relatively modest effect and removing the abstract slightly exceeds encoder change in mean neighbour replacement. Replacing mean with CLS or SEP for the four word-level BERTs reverses that latter ordering. Four representations move together in these scenarios, including the cross-model reference. The result is a sensitivity of the practical comparison, without an isolated causal effect of pooling. It gives no justification for choosing a recipe simply because it produces more agreement.""",
36: r"""The geometric extension turns to a claim a reader might actually make: that one Field has a greater measured property than another. I first check the change in articles within a model. Across the 260 model--Field cases, angular spread has no alerts under either half-sample or equal-size selection. Effective linear dimension retains four half-sample alerts, although the five equal-size selections produce none (Table S12). Both observations belong in the account. The successful equal-size check cannot stand in for the four cases flagged by the other screen.""",
37: r"""Changing the model exposes the distinction. Across all 325 Field pairs, angular spread has a persistent direction common to all ten models in 42 pairs, persistent opposition in 262 and an unresolved result in 21. For effective linear dimension, the counts are 54, 221 and 50 (Figure~\ref{fig:pairs}). In an opposition, at least two particular models maintain opposite directions over all 26 saved selection conditions. This is a claim about those persistent opposing models, not a requirement that every model disagree. An unresolved pair may still contain partial agreement.""",
38: r"""The size of the difference changes what can be made of those totals. Many angular oppositions are small. Requiring both opposing directions to exceed a symmetric relative difference of 5\% leaves 96 angular pairs; at 10\%, 19 remain. Effective linear dimension retains 177 and 126 oppositions at the same cutoffs. I keep these magnitudes beside the direction-only counts because persistent differences need not be large differences. The minimum is an explicit sensitivity choice, not a universal boundary of scientific importance. Figure~\ref{fig:pairs} keeps all 325 pairs in every row, including the growing unresolved group.""",
42: r"""Connection is where the proposed substantive interpretation failed the checks. Elongated clouds and isolated extreme points could resemble fragmentation under some rules, and the measurements changed substantially with sample size and neighbour count. The equal-size selection screen alone retained 51 connection alerts among 260 cases. I keep these results in Supplement S6 with the failed construct checks. They give no defensible basis here for saying that a Field contains more separate thematic groups.""",
43: r"""The finding I would carry into a science-mapping application is that a repeatable comparison can still depend on its representation. The centre analysis establishes broad shared structure above the random-group reference. The Field comparisons then show persistent opposite orderings under different models, despite repeated changes of articles. A global agreement score leaves that disagreement unresolved. I would therefore attach the robustness claim to the relationship, neighbour set or Field comparison being used, and follow that claim through the alternative representations.""",
44: r"""This is a continuation of the methodological question already raised in science mapping \citep{refa0d2d707e6,ref6a2f5912bc}. Evaluating a representation against an external classification, as \citet{refc0e2cc29e0} do, establishes performance relative to that criterion. Comparing alignment and retrieval, as in \citet{ref4e1e0d5582}, establishes which relationships representations share. The contribution I claim here connects those levels with the persistence and magnitude of specific Field comparisons on matched articles. It draws on existing agreement measures and an established concern with representation choice.""",
45: r"""There is a reason to be exact about what the geometric quantities mean. An angle describes separation, and PR describes how variance occupies directions. Interdisciplinarity and semantic diversity would add claims about the contents of papers. Those claims need their own evidence, as related work on embedding isotropy also cautions \citep{rudman-etal-2022-isoscore}. The fragmentation checks show why I keep this boundary explicit. A measurement can repeatedly distinguish two clouds while failing to establish the substantive difference that its name appears to promise.""",
46: r"""Before interpreting a science map, I would write down the comparison it is expected to support. For retrieval, that comparison includes the candidate papers and the number of neighbours. For Field geometry, it includes the representation rule, article balance and minimum difference. Those choices give the robustness check a concrete object. Reporting should be equally specific: a model name needs its title/abstract input, text limit and pooling rule. Otherwise, a reader knows which encoder was used but cannot reconstruct the representation from that information alone.""",
47: r"""Medicine is a useful reason to resist a single explanation of disagreement. Its shape agreement is below Energy's, yet its neighbour agreement is not consistently the lowest. Balancing Subfield composition and excluding BioBERT and PubMedBERT both leave the Medicine--Energy difference (Supplement S5). Those results constrain explanations without selecting a unique cause. Model families also shift with readout. Since these encoders differ in linked architectures, training sources and objectives, their similarities cannot disentangle those influences causally.""",
48: r"""The temporal comparison illustrates how a conclusion can turn on the comparison opportunity. Mean shape agreement rises slightly from the first period to the last. Neighbour agreement falls by 0.87 percentage points with all candidates and rises by 0.91 points with 2,048 candidates per group. The search design changes the sign. I do not read this as historical convergence of science. Current models represent publications from different dates using their current parameters; they do not recover the knowledge available at the time those publications appeared.""",
49: r"""A large corpus still has boundaries. This one contains selected OpenAlex records with usable abstracts and predominantly English text. The reinforcement sample, balanced panels and equal weights support comparisons across Fields without estimating the worldwide distribution of research. The atlas leaves visible classification and content errors that survived the cleaning rules. Removing specified problems cannot certify every record. Saved selections and frozen checkpoints let others inspect the comparison; further selections from the same corpus leave its missing coverage untouched.""",
50: r"""The model panel needs the same qualification. The encoders share some architectures, training sources and objectives, and several were not trained specifically for similarity. Checks restricted to six similarity-oriented encoders and alternative readouts help locate sensitivities within the panel, without turning it into a representative sample of all encoders. A persistent opposition establishes that the choice matters among these models. It cannot decide which of the opposing orderings is substantively right.""",
51: r"""I also retain the exploratory history. The geometry extension followed earlier results, and the all-pairs summary followed its initial examples. Making that sequence and the complete tables available does not turn the later comparisons into a confirmatory study. The next question I would pursue is whether one consequential field comparison agrees with independently assessed relationships between articles or answers a discipline-specific question. That step would test the interpretation left open here. A larger model panel alone would not supply it.""",
52: r"""A shared broad map leaves room for different neighbours and opposite geometric comparisons between Fields. Text input can matter as much as model choice, and a result that survives changing the articles may still reverse when the encoder changes. I would therefore assess robustness where the scientific claim is made. Angular spread and effective linear dimension allow that assessment for defined geometric properties, with magnitudes and processing sensitivities kept visible. The distinction that remains is essential: these are properties of the representations. Establishing what they say about scientific knowledge requires further validation.""",
}
